.TH CBMAP_CONSTRUCT_MP_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_construct_mp_scoped \- declare and create an ordered map with a custom allocator, destroyed on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_construct_mp_scoped(hm_name, key_t, val_t, mmgmt_procs);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle with the name hm_name. It also declares the
companion type variables. Those variables carry key_t and val_t to every
later type-inferred macro. The macro then creates a map that takes all of its
memory from mmgmt_procs. The macro also attaches the cleanup attribute. That
attribute destroys the map on every exit from the enclosing scope, an early
return included.

The macro is cbmap_construct_mp(3) plus that cleanup attribute. Nothing else
differs. cbmap_construct_scoped(3) is the same macro with the default
allocator. cbmap_construct_cc_scoped(3) takes a comparison procedure in
place of an allocator, and cbmap_construct_full_scoped(3) takes both.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name      Name for the map handle to declare
key_t        Key type. It tells every later macro how to read a key. It
             also tells the container which order to give the tree
val_t        Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read it. The library
             never gives it to the container
mmgmt_procs  Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, or NULL for the default
             allocator
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro declares variables and does not expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails
The macro needs GCC or Clang, because it uses the cleanup attribute
All four members of ccol_memmgmt_procs_t must not be NULL. The procedures
must stay callable for the whole life of the map. That life ends here at the
closing brace of the enclosing scope
Everything that the map allocates comes from these procedures. The
destruction at the exit of the scope returns all of it to them
The walk visits the elements in ascending key order, by the order that key_t
selects. cbmap_begin_iter(3) and cbmap_create_full(3) describe that
The exit of the scope destroys the map and clears the handle. An explicit
cbmap_destroy(3) earlier in the scope therefore stays correct
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle. A function
that receives the handle therefore needs cbmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The handle must not outlive its scope. Do not return it. Do not store it in
a place that outlives the scope. Both actions give out a handle whose map
the cleanup attribute frees at the exit of the scope.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t procs = {
    .malloc = my_malloc, .calloc = my_calloc,
    .realloc = my_realloc, .free = my_free,
};

void tally(int *ids, size_t n) {
    cbmap_construct_mp_scoped(seen, int, int, &procs);
    int one = 1;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cbmap_insert(seen, ids[i], one);
    }
}                                 /* The macro destroys seen here */
.fi
